FedMed is a healthcare system based on Federated Learning, where multiple hospitals or medical institutions can collaboratively train a machine learning model without directly sharing their patient data.

Advantages of FedMed
Data Privacy
Patient data remains within the hospital. Only model updates are shared, reducing the need to transfer sensitive medical records.
Improved Security
Since raw medical data is not centralized, the risk associated with a single large patient-data repository is reduced.
Collaborative Learning
Multiple hospitals can contribute to the development of a common ML model, allowing the model to learn from diverse datasets.
Better Model Generalization
Data from different hospitals, locations, and patient populations can help the model perform better across different healthcare environments.
Reduced Data Transfer
Instead of transferring large medical datasets, FedMed mainly exchanges model parameters or updates, which can reduce data movement.
Scalability
New hospitals or healthcare centers can be added as participating clients without requiring them to transfer their complete datasets to a central server.
Compliance Support
Keeping patient data within the original institution can make it easier to follow data-protection and healthcare privacy requirements, although compliance still depends on the complete system design.
Continuous Improvement
The global model can be periodically updated using learning from participating hospitals, allowing the system to improve over time.
Disadvantages of FedMed
Communication Overhead
Repeatedly sending model updates between hospitals and the central server can require significant network bandwidth.
High Computational Requirements
Each participating hospital needs sufficient computing resources to train the model locally.
Non-IID Data Problem
Medical data can differ significantly between hospitals because of different patient populations, equipment, treatments, and data-collection methods. This can affect model performance.
Unequal Data Quality
Some hospitals may have incomplete, noisy, or imbalanced datasets, which can affect the quality of the global model.
System Complexity
Managing multiple hospitals, local models, model aggregation, authentication, privacy, and communication makes the system more complex than centralized machine learning.
Security Risks
Although raw patient data is not shared, model updates themselves can potentially reveal information if proper privacy and security mechanisms are not implemented.
Slow or Unavailable Clients
Some hospitals may have slower hardware, poor connectivity, or temporary system failures, which can delay federated training rounds.
Implementation Cost
Deploying and maintaining the required infrastructure, security mechanisms, communication systems, and monitoring tools can require significant resources.